\usepackage[a4paper,margin=27mm]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,hyperref}
\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}
\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}
\title{Three exterior vertices in a graph of girth five}
\author{Human Compression project}
\date{13 October 2026}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
For an edge-girth-regular graph of degree $k\geq4$, order $k^2+4$
and girth five, with $(k-1)^2-3$ pentagons through every edge,
the three vertices outside a Moore ball are independent.
Their incidences with the ball have only five possibilities up to
relabelling, independently of $k$. At degree four, completing these
five configurations proves the uniqueness of the graph on twenty
vertices with six pentagons through each edge.
\end{abstract}

\section{The common reduction}
Graphs are finite, simple and undirected. A \emph{pentagon} is a
cycle of length five, counted without orientation or a distinguished
starting vertex. Edge-girth-regularity here means that the graph is
regular, has girth five, and has a constant number $\lambda$ of
pentagons through each edge.

\begin{lemma}\label{exterior}
Let $k\geq4$, and let $G$ be edge-girth-regular of degree $k$,
order $k^2+4$, girth five and $\lambda=(k-1)^2-3$.
Choose a vertex $r$. The three vertices outside its Moore ball
are independent, and each meets exactly one leaf in each of its
$k$ branches.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The root $r$, its $k$ neighbours, and the $k-1$ further neighbours
of each of those vertices are all distinct. A collision would give
a triangle or a quadrangle. They form a rooted tree on
$1+k+k(k-1)=k^2+1$ vertices, with $k$ branches of $k-1$ leaves
each. Its internal vertices already have degree $k$.
Thus every further edge between these vertices joins leaves.
Leaves in the same branch cannot be adjacent, since their common
parent would give a triangle.

Every pentagon through $r$ uses exactly one edge between leaves in
different branches, and conversely each such edge gives exactly
one pentagon through $r$. There are $k\lambda/2$ pentagons through
$r$, since each uses two of its incident edges. The leaves have
$k(k-1)^2$ degree slots beyond their parent edges. Their mutual
edges occupy $k\lambda$ slots, leaving
\[
                  k\bigl((k-1)^2-\lambda\bigr)=3k
\]
incidences with the exterior set $Y$ of three vertices.
These fill all $3k$ degrees of $Y$, so $Y$ is independent.
A vertex of $Y$ meets at most one leaf in a branch, since two
would give a quadrangle through their parent. Its degree $k$
therefore forces exactly one neighbour in every branch.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{five}
Up to relabelling, the incidences between the Moore ball and its
exterior in Lemma~\ref{exterior} belong to a list of five explicit forms.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Label the exterior vertices $0,1,2$ temporarily. In any branch
they each choose one leaf. Partition $\{0,1,2\}$ according to
which vertices choose the same leaf. A branch therefore gives
the discrete partition, one of the three partitions with a pair
and a singleton, or the single class of size three.
No pair of exterior vertices can occur in the same class in two
branches, since that would give two common neighbours and hence
a quadrangle.

If some branch contains the class of size three, every other
branch must have the discrete partition. This is one form.
Otherwise there are $j$ branches with a pair and a singleton,
where $0\leq j\leq3$, and the $j$ pairs must be distinct.
Every other branch has the discrete partition. Permuting the
three exterior vertices gives every permutation of the three
possible pairs; permuting branches then identifies all choices
with the same $j$. These are the other four forms.

Within a branch, permuting its leaves identifies any two
realizations of its partition, including the unused leaves.
There are enough leaves to realize every form, since $k-1\geq3$.
Consequently the five forms account for the actual incidences,
with no additional unresolved assignment of leaf labels.
They need not all extend to regular graphs.
\end{proof}

\section{Completion in degree four}
At $k=4$ the Moore ball has seventeen vertices: a root, its four
neighbours, and twelve leaves arranged in four triples. Add the
three exterior vertices according to each of the five forms.
The only undecided edges join leaves. A leaf adjacent to $d$
exterior vertices needs $3-d$ further neighbours among the leaves.
The root, its neighbours and all exterior vertices already have
their final degrees.

The finite completion procedure chooses a deficient leaf $v$.
It lists every other deficient leaf $w$ such that adding $vw$
creates neither a triangle nor a quadrangle. If $v$ needs $s$
edges, it tries all subsets of $s$ candidates in increasing label
order, checking the girth condition after each added edge.
It then repeats with another deficient leaf. A branch fails if
fewer than $s$ candidates remain. At a complete graph, it counts
the pentagons through every edge and retains the graph precisely
when every count is six.

For completeness, fix any completion of the current partial graph.
All its missing neighbours of $v$ belong to the candidate list,
because a forbidden short cycle could not disappear later.
Adding those neighbours in label order remains a subgraph of the
completion and therefore satisfies every local test. One branch
thus reaches it. Repeating this argument proves completeness by
induction on the missing edges. A leaf whose required edges have
been added is full and can receive no later edge, so each complete
labelled graph follows just one branch. Every step adds an edge
and the total number of edges is bounded, proving termination.

The implementation tests whether an added edge creates a forbidden
cycle by searching for an existing path of length at most three
between its endpoints. The final pentagon count enumerates simple
paths of length four between the endpoints of each edge. Such a
path cannot use that edge; adding it closes exactly one pentagon.
Choosing a leaf with few surplus candidates is only a search heuristic.
No automorphism pruning or graph-generation library is needed.

The complete results, where a node is one call of the completion
procedure, are
\[
\begin{array}{c|r|r|r}
\text{form}&\text{nodes}&\text{complete graphs}&\text{retained graphs}\\\hline
j=0&61&6&6\\
j=1&4&0&0\\
j=2&40&0&0\\
j=3&55&0&0\\
\text{one triple}&159&12&12
\end{array}
\]
These eighteen labelled graphs all have literal verified
isomorphisms to the source example \nolinkurl{UntilOrder250_82}.
The full adjacency lists and bijections are included in the
certificate. Proposing a bijection may use a graph-isomorphism
routine; its acceptance checks every pair of vertices directly.
All five searches terminate completely, and both ordinary and
sanitized builds give identical adjacency lists and node counts.

\begin{theorem}
The minimum order of an edge-girth-regular graph with
$(k,g,\lambda)=(4,5,6)$ is twenty, and exactly one isomorphism
class occurs at that order.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A quartic graph of girth five has at least seventeen vertices by
the Moore ball. Counting incidences between edges and pentagons
gives $5\mid |E(G)|\lambda=12|V(G)|$, so its order is divisible
by five and is at least twenty. At order twenty, the lemmas and
the complete searches give at most one isomorphism class.
The retained graph is connected, is quartic and has no triangles
or quadrangles. Its verified six pentagons through every edge
give girth exactly five and prove existence.
\end{proof}

\section{Attribution and scope}
This is the uniqueness entry in Table~2, physical page~18, of
Goedgebeur and Jooken~\cite{GJ}. The table attributes the earlier
lower bound to Drglin, Filipovski, Jajcay and Raiman, its
reference~[8, Theorem~2.3]. Its literature upper-bound column is
left infinite; the finite example and uniqueness assertion are
in the current-paper column. Those distinctions are retained.
The Moore ball, cycle-incidence counts and exhaustive
completion are established methods. Our contribution is the
explicit reduction to five forms and the small complete certificate
for this entry; we assert no historical priority for the graph or
its uniqueness.

The common lemmas also apply to the source parameters
$(n,k,g,\lambda)=(40,6,5,22)$. They reduce that case to the same
five incidence forms, with five leaves per branch. The present
certificate completes only the degree-four case and makes no
classification claim for degree six.

